{
  "id": "T18_42_an_expectations_transitions_under_an_unavailable_interpretation_are_read_as_recorded_and_unverified",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.6.14",
    "7.6.13",
    "7.6.9",
    "9.5.8",
    "I-17",
    "10.2.9",
    "9.3.1",
    "9.5.7",
    "7.6.10",
    "4.6.1"
  ],
  "description": "An expectation requires A-601 to show \"90 min\" and A-601's Tuesday issue shows \"60 min\". While no normalization applies, an application's fulfillment end act citing that Assertion differs from the derivation and is rejected. A normalization of the project, effective Friday, names the fixture fire-rating interpretation; the property stays open under it, so its write records nothing. A retire_interpretation step then removes the interpretation: the transition check reports no difference and lists the property's begin act as unverified. A-601's Saturday \"1.5 h\" would fulfil the property under the interpretation; the write records nothing, and the application's fulfillment end act at Saturday, matched_by the normalization, is accepted without comparison. A later \"90 min\" revises nothing. Reads that evaluate the expectation under its own interpretation read it as recorded: outstanding on Tuesday, fulfilled now. A Saturday frame requesting literal comparison computes it: outstanding. A frame naming the retired interpretation gets the limitation. An expectation whose context names an application interpretation is first recorded with a limitation begin act; the application then revises it and writes its fulfillment, both accepted without comparison. Frames naming that interpretation, or none, evaluate it under its own and read those acts: fulfilled.",
  "note": "A normalization naming an interpretation the reference does not have is refused (10.2.9), so the fixture normalizes under a fixture interpretation and then retires it (README section 2, `retire_interpretation`). 7.6.14: \"*Recorded results are read as recorded.* A read that evaluates the unit under its own interpretation, including one whose frame names that interpretation, reads the unit's recorded transitions as recorded\"; a read \"evaluates an expectation under its own when its frame names no interpretation, whether the expectation's context names one or not\", so step 27 reads Door 102's expectation from its recorded acts. README section 5, Limitations: \"An expectation read under its own interpretation is read as recorded, whether or not the reference has that interpretation\". 10.2.9 compares an explicit baseline act with the derivation only \"where the unit's interpretation is available (7.6.14)\". A revise is not a begin or end act, so app-rev-2 is not listed as unverified.",
